"""Original conversation lessons added to the initial catalog; append to preserve IDs."""
rows = [
('plans','look for','Try to find someone or something.','人や物を探す。','You need a quiet place to work for an hour. Ask the hotel receptionist.',"I'm looking for somewhere quiet to take a call. Is there a room I could use?",'Your friend asks what kind of job you want next.',"I'm looking for a role where I can work closely with customers.",'Look for something / look for it','Keep the object after for: look for it. Looking for describes the search, not a successful result.','Look for searches for something; look into investigates a question.'),
('work','look into','Investigate a problem or possibility.','問題や可能性を調べる。','A customer says their invoice is wrong. Promise to investigate.',"I'll look into the charge and get back to you tomorrow.",'Your partner suggests a different internet provider. Offer to research it.',"That sounds promising. I'll look into it this weekend.",'Look into something / look into it','The object stays after into. Useful for offering an investigation without promising a particular result.','Look into investigates; look through examines a set of materials.'),
('connect','look after','Take care of someone or something.','人や物の世話をする。','Your friend is going away and is worried about their cat. Offer help.',"I can look after your cat while you're away.",'A colleague needs to leave an event briefly. Offer to watch their belongings.',"Go ahead. I'll look after your bag.",'Look after someone / look after it','Keep the object after after. Can mean ongoing care or temporary responsibility.','Look after takes care of someone; look out for someone can mean watching for their arrival.'),
('work','look up','Find information in a reference source.','辞書などで情報を調べる。','You cannot remember a technical term in a discussion. Explain what you will do.',"Give me a moment to look that term up.",'A friend asks when the last train leaves. You do not know.',"I'm not sure, but I can look it up.",'Look something up / look it up','Separable: look the word up or look up the word, but look it up. Name a source with in or on.','Look up retrieves a specific fact; look into investigates a broader issue.'),
('connect','look forward to','Feel pleased about something that will happen.','これからのことを楽しみにする。','You have arranged dinner with an old friend. End your message warmly.',"I'm really looking forward to catching up on Friday.",'A new teammate emails before their first day. Welcome them.',"We're looking forward to working with you.",'Look forward to something / to doing something','To is a preposition here: to seeing you, not to see you. Warm and common in both speech and email.','Look forward to anticipates something with pleasure; expect makes a prediction.'),
('plans','look out for','Watch carefully so you notice someone or something.','見逃さないように気をつけて見る。','Your friend is picking you up at a busy station. Help them spot you.',"Look out for a blue backpack. I'll be by the main entrance.",'Your teammate is waiting for an important email. Remind them.',"Look out for a message from the venue this afternoon.",'Look out for someone / something','Keep the complete sequence together: look out for it. This lesson concerns noticing, rather than protecting someone.','Look out for watches for a person or sign; look after takes responsibility for care.'),
('connect','look up to','Admire and respect someone.','人を尊敬する。','A colleague asks why your former manager mattered to you.',"I looked up to her because she stayed calm when things went wrong.",'A friend says their younger sibling copies everything they do. Offer a kind interpretation.',"It sounds like he really looks up to you.",'Look up to someone / look up to her','The person follows to. Often expresses respect for someone you see as a role model.','Look up to admires a person; look up finds information.'),
('perspective','look back on','Think about a past experience.','過去の出来事を振り返る。','A friend asks whether leaving your old job was the right choice.',"Looking back on it, I think I stayed longer than I needed to.",'You are discussing a difficult first year abroad.',"I look back on that year with more affection now.",'Look back on something / look back on it','Keep on before the experience. Add with regret, pride, or affection to describe your present feelings.','Look back on reflects on the past; look forward to anticipates a future event with pleasure.'),
('work','look through','Examine the contents of something.','資料などに目を通す。','Your colleague sends a long report and asks for comments today.',"I'll look through the report after lunch and flag anything unclear.",'A friend wants to find a photo from an old trip. Offer to help.',"Let's look through the album together.",'Look through something / look through it','The object follows through. Can suggest a scan; use read carefully if a close reading matters.','Look through examines material; look for searches for a particular item.'),
('perspective','look down on','Consider someone or something less worthy of respect.','人や物を見下す。','Someone dismisses a friend because of their job. Challenge the attitude.',"I don't think we should look down on people because of the work they do.",'A friend worries about being judged for not attending university. Reassure them.',"Anyone who looks down on you for that is missing the point.",'Look down on someone / look down on them','A negative judgment, often about status. Keep the object after on; use cautiously when describing someone’s attitude.','Look down on expresses contempt; disagree with challenges a view without implying contempt.'),
('work','get back to','Contact someone again with a reply or information.','返答や情報を持って改めて連絡する。','You need to check availability before accepting a meeting. Respond.',"Can I get back to you after I've checked my calendar?",'A friend asks for advice while you are rushing out. Promise a later reply.',"I want to give this some thought. I'll get back to you tonight.",'Get back to someone / get back to you','The person follows to. A specific time makes the promise more useful.','Get back to someone replies later; get back returns to a place or activity.'),
('perspective','get around to','Finally do something after a delay.','後回しにしていたことをようやくする。','A friend asks whether you read the book they recommended. Admit the delay.',"I haven't got around to reading it yet, but it's next on my list.",'You finally repaired a shelf after months. Tell your housemate.',"I finally got around to fixing that shelf.",'Get around to something / to doing something','To takes a noun or -ing form. Often acknowledges that a task has been postponed.','Get around to finally starts a delayed task; put off deliberately delays it.'),
('connect','get along with','Have a friendly relationship with someone.','人とうまく付き合う。','A friend asks about your new housemate.',"We get along with each other pretty well so far.",'You are asked whether two colleagues will work well together.',"They have different styles, but they get along with each other.",'Get along with someone / get along with them','Keep with before the person. Common in American English; get on with also describes a good relationship.','Get along with describes a relationship; catch up with exchanges news after time apart.'),
('perspective','get over','Recover from a difficult experience or illness.','つらい経験や病気から立ち直る。','A friend asks why you are taking things slowly after being ill.',"I'm still getting over that cold, so I'll have an early night.",'Someone expects a friend to recover quickly from a breakup. Respond with care.',"It may take her a while to get over it.",'Get over something / get over it','The object follows over. The command get over it can sound dismissive; avoid it when someone needs support.','Get over emphasizes recovery; get through emphasizes enduring a difficult period.'),
('work','get through','Finish dealing with a quantity of work.','一定量の仕事などを終える。','Your manager asks how much of the backlog you can handle today.',"I should be able to get through these requests by five.",'A friend asks why you cannot join lunch yet.',"I've got a few more emails to get through first.",'Get through something / get through it','The object stays after through. This sense concerns completing a workload; getting through to someone has a different meaning.','Get through finishes a workload; go through may examine it without finishing the task.'),
('perspective','get away with','Avoid a bad consequence despite doing something wrong.','悪いことをしても罰や不利益を免れる。','A colleague repeatedly takes credit for other people’s work. Express concern.',"He keeps getting away with taking credit for the whole team.",'A friend asks whether they can skip a required check. Discourage it.',"You might get away with it once, but I wouldn't risk it.",'Get away with something / with doing something','With takes a noun or -ing form. Often implies unfairness or risk, not approval.','Get away with avoids consequences; get away leaves or escapes.'),
('work','get by','Manage with limited money, knowledge, or resources.','十分でない条件でも何とかやっていく。','A colleague worries that the team has only two people this week.',"We can get by with two people if we postpone the nonessential work.",'A friend asks how much Italian you speak.',"Just enough to get by when I'm travelling.",'Get by / get by with something / on an amount','Can stand alone. With introduces a resource; on commonly introduces money available.','Get by means manage adequately; get ahead suggests making progress beyond that.'),
]
rows += [
('work','take on','Accept a task or responsibility.','仕事や責任を引き受ける。','Your manager offers you another project when you are already busy. Set a boundary.',"I can't take on another project unless we move one of my deadlines.",'Your friend is organizing a party alone. Offer a specific kind of help.',"I can take on the food if you handle the invitations.",'Take something on / take it on','Separable. Often implies responsibility or effort; taking something on is not the same as finishing it.','Take on accepts responsibility; take over assumes responsibility from someone else.'),
('work','take over','Assume control or responsibility from someone else.','人に代わって仕事や役割を引き継ぐ。','Your teammate is leaving early. Offer to lead the rest of the meeting.',"I can take over from here. Send me your notes when you have a moment.",'Your partner has been driving for hours. Offer a break.',"Would you like me to take over for a while?",'Take over / take something over / take over from someone','Can stand alone or take an object: take it over. From identifies the previous person in charge.','Take over replaces someone in a role; take on accepts a new responsibility.'),
('perspective','take in','Understand and mentally absorb information.','情報を理解して受け止める。','You have just heard several big changes to a plan. Ask for a moment.',"That's a lot to take in. Can we go through the main points again?",'A friend shares surprising news and asks what you think.',"I'm still taking it in, to be honest.",'Take something in / take it in','Separable. Often refers to information that is complex, unexpected, or emotionally significant.','Take in absorbs information; take on accepts a task or responsibility.'),
('connect','take up','Start doing an activity regularly.','趣味や活動を始める。','A friend asks what you do outside work these days.',"I've taken up swimming again. It helps me switch off.",'Your partner wants a hobby you can share. Suggest one.',"Why don't we take up something we can do together, like dancing?",'Take up an activity / take it up','Separable. Usually used with a hobby, sport, or regular interest; this lesson is not the occupy-time sense.','Take up starts an activity; keep up continues an existing practice or pace.'),
('connect','take back','Withdraw something you previously said.','前に言ったことを撤回する。','You realize you judged a colleague’s idea too quickly. Correct yourself.',"I take back what I said. Your approach solves the problem more clearly.",'A friend is hurt by an unfair comment you made. Apologize.',"That was unfair, and I take it back. I'm sorry.",'Take something back / take it back','Separable. A direct apology may still be needed; withdrawing the words does not undo their effect.','Take back retracts a statement; take in absorbs information.'),
('plans','take off','Leave the ground and begin flying.','飛行機などが離陸する。','A friend asks whether you can call once you are on the plane.',"Probably not. We're due to take off in a few minutes.",'Your flight has been delayed. Update the person meeting you.',"We haven't taken off yet. I'll message you when we land.",'The plane takes off / we take off','No direct object in this flight sense. Take your coat off is a separate, transitive sense.','Take off is departure from the ground; land is arrival on the ground.'),
('perspective','put up with','Accept an unpleasant situation without stopping it.','嫌なことを我慢する。','A friend says their neighbour plays loud music every night. Offer support.',"You shouldn't have to put up with that every night.",'A teammate asks why you are changing offices.',"I've put up with the noise for months, and I need somewhere quieter.",'Put up with something / put up with it','Keep all three words together. With takes a noun or -ing form; the phrase suggests the situation is unwelcome.','Put up with tolerates a problem; deal with can mean taking action to resolve it.'),
('work','put forward','Offer an idea for others to consider.','案や意見を提案する。','Your team has discussed only one solution. Offer an alternative.',"Could I put forward a different approach before we decide?",'A community group is choosing an event. Make a suggestion.',"I'd like to put forward the idea of a smaller, local event.",'Put something forward / put it forward','Separable. More deliberate and slightly more formal than suggest, but usable in a meeting.','Put forward proposes an idea; bring up introduces a topic for discussion.'),
('work','put together','Create something by combining its parts.','要素をまとめて資料や計画などを作る。','A colleague asks what you can prepare before the client call.',"I'll put together a short summary of the options.",'Your friend needs a travel plan but feels overwhelmed. Offer help.',"Let's put an itinerary together this weekend.",'Put something together / put it together','Separable. Used for practical outputs such as a plan, team, presentation, or meal.','Put together assembles a result; think through examines how an idea would work.'),
('work','put aside','Temporarily stop considering a concern or disagreement.','問題や意見の違いをいったん脇に置く。','Two teammates keep returning to an old argument. Refocus the meeting.',"Can we put that disagreement aside and decide what the client needs now?",'A friend is struggling to make a decision because of an old disappointment.',"If you put that experience aside for a moment, what would you choose?",'Put something aside / put it aside','Separable. Signals temporary suspension, not that the concern is invalid or permanently resolved.','Put aside suspends consideration; rule out removes an option from consideration.'),
('connect','put down','Make someone feel less capable or important.','人をけなして自信を失わせる。','A friend’s partner repeatedly mocks their ambitions. Name the behaviour gently.',"It sounds like he keeps putting you down when you talk about your plans.",'A colleague says they were only joking after a hurtful remark. Set a boundary.',"You can disagree with me without putting me down.",'Put someone down / put them down','Separable. This sense describes belittling, not physically placing something on a surface.','Put down belittles a person; turn down declines an offer or request.'),
('work','put through','Connect someone to another person by phone.','電話を相手につなぐ。','You have reached reception and need the accounts team. Ask to be connected.',"Could you put me through to accounts, please?",'A caller needs your colleague. Offer to connect them.',"Hold on a moment. I'll put you through to Maya.",'Put someone through to someone / put me through','Place the person between put and through. To introduces the destination of the call.','Put someone through connects a call; get through to someone succeeds in reaching them.'),
('work','come up with','Think of an idea or solution.','案や解決策を思いつく。','Your team is stuck on a scheduling problem. Offer a possible solution.',"I've come up with a way to give everyone a free afternoon.",'A friend asks whether you have chosen a gift.',"Not yet. I'm trying to come up with something more personal.",'Come up with something / come up with it','Keep the object after with. Highlights producing the idea, without saying it is necessarily the best solution.','Come up with produces an idea; come up means a topic or situation arises.'),
('perspective','come around','Change an opinion and become more willing to agree.','考えを変えて賛成するようになる。','A colleague rejected a proposal yesterday but now seems interested. Explain the change.',"She's coming around to the idea now that she's seen the costs.",'A friend is upset that their parents dislike their plan. Respond without guaranteeing agreement.',"They might come around if you give them time to think about it.",'Come around / come around to an idea','Can stand alone. To introduces the view someone begins to accept. Come round is also used, especially in British English.','Come around changes a view; come across creates an impression or encounters something, depending on the sense.'),
('work','come down to','Have one main deciding factor.','結局はある要因にかかっている。','Your team is choosing between two equally strong candidates. Identify the deciding issue.',"I think it comes down to who can join us sooner.",'A friend asks whether you will move to a bigger flat.',"It comes down to whether we can afford the extra rent.",'Come down to something / to whether…','Keep the object or clause after to. Often summarizes a decision without denying other considerations.','Come down to identifies the decisive factor; come up with produces an idea.'),
('plans','come along','Go somewhere together with someone.','一緒について行く。','You are going to a market and want to include a friend.',"We're going to the market on Sunday. Would you like to come along?",'Your partner thinks an invitation is just for you. Clarify.',"They said you could come along too.",'Come along / come along with someone','No direct object in this invitation sense. Usually casual and inclusive; with can name the companion.','Come along joins someone on an outing; get along describes managing or a relationship.'),
]
rows += [
('work','go through','Examine or discuss something carefully from start to finish.','内容を順に確認する。','Your colleague is about to send a contract. Suggest checking the details together.',"Let's go through the terms once more before you send it.",'A friend is confused by a complicated bill. Offer to help.',"We can go through it line by line if that helps.",'Go through something / go through it','The object follows through. This lesson concerns examination, not experiencing hardship or using supplies.','Go through examines systematically; look through can suggest a quicker scan.'),
('plans','go ahead','Proceed with a plan or action.','計画や行動を進める。','Your team has resolved the last issue with an event. Confirm the next step.',"We can go ahead with the event now that the venue is confirmed.",'A friend asks whether they should order while you are running late.',"Go ahead and order. I'll be there soon.",'Go ahead / go ahead with something / go ahead and do something','With introduces a plan; and can introduce an action. Go ahead alone can give permission.','Go ahead proceeds; hold off delays action.'),
('perspective','go along with','Agree to support a suggestion or decision.','提案や決定に同意して従う。','You prefer another option but can accept the group’s plan. Respond.',"I can go along with that, as long as we review it next month.",'A friend asks why you joined a trip you were unsure about.',"I went along with the plan because everyone else seemed excited.",'Go along with something / go along with it','The object follows with. Can suggest cooperation without strong personal enthusiasm.','Go along with agrees to a plan; come along joins an outing.'),
('perspective','bring about','Cause a change or result.','変化や結果をもたらす。','A colleague asks whether a small process change will make any difference.',"It won't bring about a transformation overnight, but it should reduce delays.",'A friend asks what led to your change of attitude.',"Working with that team brought about a real change in how I listen.",'Bring something about / bring it about','Separable, though bring about a change is a common order. Often used with changes or consequences.','Bring about causes a result; bring up introduces a topic.'),
('connect','bring back','Make someone remember something.','記憶や気持ちを呼び起こす。','A song from your school days starts playing. Tell your friend what it reminds you of.',"This song brings back so many memories of that summer.",'You visit a café where you used to meet friends.',"Being here brings it all back.",'Bring something back / bring it back','Separable. This lesson uses memories and feelings as the object; returning an item is another sense.','Bring back revives a memory; look back on deliberately reflects on the past.'),
('work','bring in','Ask someone to join a task for their help or expertise.','専門家などに加わってもらう。','Your team lacks the expertise to solve a technical problem. Suggest outside help.',"We should bring in someone who has dealt with this before.",'A colleague asks whether they should join a sensitive discussion.',"Let's bring you in once we've agreed on the main questions.",'Bring someone in / bring them in','Separable. Emphasizes inviting someone into a piece of work; explain the role to avoid implying a takeover.','Bring in adds expertise or help; take over assumes control.'),
('work','bring forward','Move an event to an earlier time.','予定を早める。','The client needs a decision sooner than expected. Ask to move the meeting.',"Could we bring the meeting forward to Tuesday?",'A friend’s visit needs to happen before you move. Suggest a change.',"Would it be possible to bring your visit forward by a week?",'Bring something forward / bring it forward','Separable. To gives the new date; by gives the amount of change. This scheduling usage is especially common in British English.','Bring forward makes an event earlier; put off postpones it.'),
('plans','turn up','Arrive or appear, sometimes unexpectedly.','姿を見せる、現れる。','A friend asks whether many people attended your informal event.',"More people turned up than we expected.",'Someone you invited still has not arrived. Explain your concern.',"He said he'd be here by six, but he hasn't turned up yet.",'Someone turns up / turns up at a place','No direct object in this arrival sense. Turn the volume up is a different, separable sense.','Turn up arrives or appears; turn down declines an offer.'),
('perspective','turn out','Prove to have a particular result or character.','結果としてそうだと分かる。','You were nervous about dinner with your new neighbours. Describe how it went.',"It turned out to be a really relaxed evening.",'A colleague asks whether the error was serious.',"It turns out that we used an old version of the file.",'Turn out to be… / it turns out that…','Useful for a discovery or a result different from what was expected. No direct object in this pattern.','Turn out reveals the outcome; work out can describe a plan succeeding or solving a problem.'),
('work','turn to','Ask someone or use something for help.','助けを求めて人や物に頼る。','A new teammate asks who can help with a difficult customer issue.',"You can turn to Priya if you need a second opinion.",'A friend asks how you handled a stressful move.',"I turned to a few friends who had been through the same thing.",'Turn to someone / turn to something for help','The object follows to. Can refer to a person, service, or resource that provides support.','Turn to seeks help; reach out initiates contact more generally.'),
('perspective','turn around','Change an unsuccessful situation so it improves.','悪い状況を好転させる。','A colleague thinks a struggling project cannot recover. Offer a practical view.',"We can still turn this project around if we narrow the scope.",'A friend says their café is finally doing better. Ask what changed.',"What helped you turn things around?",'Turn something around / turn it around','Separable. Suggests a substantial improvement, rather than a minor adjustment.','Turn around improves a poor situation; turn out describes the eventual result.'),
('perspective','give up','Stop trying to do something.','努力や試みを諦める。','Your friend is discouraged by a difficult course. Encourage a smaller next step.',"Don't give up on it yet. Could you try one lesson with a tutor?",'A colleague asks whether you solved an old software problem.',"I gave up trying to fix it myself and asked for help.",'Give up / give up doing something / give up on something','Can stand alone. A verb immediately after give up takes -ing; on introduces the thing or person you stop believing in.','Give up stops trying; put off delays a task without necessarily abandoning it.'),
('connect','give in','Stop resisting and agree to a demand.','抵抗をやめて要求を受け入れる。','Your friend keeps being pressured into weekend work. Support their boundary.',"You don't have to give in just because they keep asking.",'A colleague asks how a long negotiation ended.',"We gave in on the delivery date, but kept the original price.",'Give in / give in to pressure / give in on an issue','No direct object. To introduces pressure or the person making demands; on can identify the issue conceded.','Give in yields to pressure; give up abandons an effort.'),
('connect','give away','Reveal information that was meant to stay secret.','秘密や答えをうっかり明かす。','Your friend is about to discuss the ending of a film you have not seen. Stop them warmly.',"Don't give away the ending. I'm watching it tonight.",'A surprise party is being planned. Warn a friend to be discreet.',"Try not to give it away when you talk to her.",'Give something away / give it away','Separable. Often used for secrets, answers, or plot details; donating an item is another sense.','Give away reveals a secret; get across communicates an intended message.'),
('work','set up','Arrange or establish something so it can happen.','会議や仕組みなどを準備して整える。','A colleague suggests discussing a problem with the whole team. Offer to organize it.',"I'll set up a short call for tomorrow morning.",'A friend wants a regular way to share travel plans.',"We could set up a group chat just for the trip.",'Set something up / set it up','Separable. Covers arrangements and practical systems; specify what you are establishing.','Set up creates the arrangement; wrap up brings the activity to an end.'),
('plans','set aside','Reserve time, money, or another resource for a purpose.','目的のために時間やお金などを取っておく。','You keep missing time to practise speaking. Tell a friend about your new plan.',"I'm setting aside ten minutes each morning to speak out loud.",'Your partner asks how you will pay for an unexpected repair.',"We've set some money aside for things like this.",'Set something aside / set it aside for something','Separable. For names a purpose; to can introduce the action you plan to take.','Set aside reserves a resource; put aside a disagreement suspends attention to it.'),
('perspective','set out','Explain ideas or plans clearly and in an organized way.','考えや計画を順序立てて説明する。','A colleague’s proposal is difficult to follow. Ask for a clearer explanation.',"Could you set out the main steps before we discuss the details?",'A friend is preparing to make a difficult request. Suggest a structure.',"Start by setting out what you need and why.",'Set something out / set it out','Separable. Slightly formal, useful when clarity or structure matters. Set out on a journey is a different sense.','Set out explains a plan clearly; set up makes the practical arrangements.'),
('connect','run into','Meet someone unexpectedly.','人に偶然会う。','You unexpectedly met an old colleague at a café. Tell a friend.',"I ran into Sam this morning. He's moved back to the city.",'You see someone from a former class at an event. Start a conversation.',"I didn't expect to run into you here. How have you been?",'Run into someone / run into them','The person follows into. Informal; run into a problem uses a related but different object and meaning.','Run into meets someone by chance; catch up with exchanges news, often by arrangement.'),
('plans','run out of','Use all of a supply so none remains.','必要な物を使い切る、なくなる。','You are cooking with a friend and discover there is no milk left. Explain.',"We've run out of milk. Would oat milk work instead?",'Your team is still debating just before a deadline. Refocus them.',"We're running out of time. Can we agree on the next step?",'Run out of something / run out of it','Keep of before the supply. The time or resource can also be the subject: our time is running out.','Run out of has none left; get by manages with what is available.'),
('work','run by','Tell someone an idea to get their opinion.','意見をもらうために案を人に話す。','You want feedback on a proposal before presenting it. Ask a colleague.',"Can I run an idea by you before the meeting?",'A friend suggests an expensive shared trip. Ask for time to discuss it at home.',"Let me run it by my partner first.",'Run something by someone / run it by you','Put the idea before by and the person after it. Conversational; often asks for a quick reaction rather than a formal review.','Run by seeks someone’s opinion; get across focuses on making the idea understood.'),
('plans','hold off','Wait before taking an action.','行動をしばらく控える。','Prices may change next week. Suggest delaying a purchase.',"Let's hold off on booking until we know the dates for sure.",'A teammate wants to send an announcement before approval arrives. Ask them to wait.',"Could you hold off for a day? We're still waiting for the final numbers.",'Hold off / hold off on doing something','Can stand alone; on introduces a noun or -ing form. The delay is usually temporary.','Hold off waits before acting; rule out excludes an option.'),
('plans','hold up','Delay someone or something.','人や物事の進行を遅らせる。','Your colleague asks why you are late to the call. Explain briefly.',"Sorry I'm late. I was held up by a problem with the train.",'A client asks why a delivery has not arrived. Explain the cause.',"A missing document is holding up the shipment.",'Hold someone or something up / hold it up','Separable; often passive for an unexpected delay. This lesson is not the robbery sense.','Hold up causes a delay; hold off means choosing to wait.'),
('connect','hold on','Wait for a short time.','少し待つ。','A friend calls while you are looking for your headphones. Ask for a moment.',"Can you hold on a second while I find my headphones?",'A colleague starts closing a shared document before you finish. Stop them politely.',"Hold on a moment. I haven't saved my changes yet.",'Hold on / hold on a moment','No direct object in this waiting sense. A gentle tone or can you makes it less abrupt.','Hold on asks for a brief wait; hold off usually delays an action or decision.'),
]
